    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1448">

                <pb facs="1170" n="1054" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg1054"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.00/2046.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the Secretary of
                    State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-10-01T09:00:00-05:45">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 1, 1912—9 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your September 26, 6 p.m. I believe that any other course than the one therein
                    proposed would result only in further disorder, which would probably spread to
                    other Central American States.</p>
                <p>No objection has ever been made to the validity of President Díaz’s succession to
                    office, and the acts complained of during his administration were Mena’s acts as
                    the real ruler of Nicaragua.</p>
                <p>The President of Salvador is misinformed as to the conduct of the United States
                    forces in Nicaragua, and if he says that the selection of Calderón as
                    provisional president is acceptable to Díaz, he misstates the facts.</p>
                <p>That Araujo is intriguing to name the next President of Nicaragua seems to me
                    certain; his idea is doubtless a Central American union under the auspices of
                    Salvador. His pretensions, I am sure, are not supported by any of the other
                    countries.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Weitzel.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
